From dae2d14ccced6072a7cc763de4c5d27be733448c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tobias Brick <tobiasb@microsoft.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 16:02:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] azl: config-driven FIPS provider drop-in activation

Replace Fedora's hard-coded FIPS force-load (fips_local.cnf / implicit
"fips") with drop-in-driven selection. When the kernel is in FIPS mode,
select the designated FIPS provider from the standard configuration --
already parsed via openssl.cnf's openssl.d include, so no config file is
opened by path here -- then activate it plus the base provider and set
the default property query.

The parts of the configuration we consult:

  # [provider_sect] and its "fips = fips_sect" entry are stock OpenSSL
  # provider registration, not introduced here. Our only tie-in is that
  # the designated provider name below must match one of these keys,
  # which is how we resolve it to a provider config section.
  [provider_sect]
  fips = fips_sect

  [azl_fips_provider]
  # Designate the FIPS provider: the key is the provider name (matching
  # its [provider_sect] entry above) and the value is the fixed marker
  # "azl-fips-module". Exactly one such entry must exist across all
  # installed provider drop-ins; zero or more than one is rejected.
  fips = azl-fips-module

  # Property-query strictness once the FIPS provider is active:
  #   enforcing -> fips=yes    preferred -> ?fips=yes
  # Absent defaults to enforcing.
  azl_fips_prop_query_mode = enforcing

When the kernel is in FIPS mode and the designated provider cannot be
activated (no designation, more than one, unregistered provider,
identity alias collision, ambiguous or invalid property-query value, or
provider load failure), initialization fails closed regardless of the
config's config_diagnostics setting. OpenSSL
otherwise swallows a config-module failure under
CONF_MFLAGS_IGNORE_RETURN_CODES when config_diagnostics is off; forcing
diagnostics on in this path makes the failure propagate out of
OPENSSL_init_crypto() rather than letting OpenSSL come up without FIPS
enforcement.

Force the designated provider active from its configured module path
rather than by bare name. provider_conf_load() reports success even when
a configured activate=1 soft-fails (it ends `return ok >= 0`, and a
non-fatal activation failure yields ok==0), so a bare-name forced
activation could fall back to loading a same-named module from the
default module directory -- silently substituting a different, possibly
non-validated module for the one the drop-in designated. Resolve the
drop-in's `module` path and pin it in the single forced
provider_conf_activate(), treating any non-success as fatal, so a module
that cannot be loaded fails closed instead of being bypassed.

Reject configurations where another providers-section entry aliases its
identity to the designated FIPS provider name. Such an entry would
activate first under the designated name, causing Fedora's
already-activated shortcut to report success without loading the pinned
module path.

The forced activation carries the designated provider section and
ambient configuration into the pinned load. This preserves integrity,
security, and other provider parameters if a configured activate=1
attempt soft-failed and the forced activation becomes a retry.

Covered by test/recipes/03-test_prov_conf_fips_* : designation,
fail-closed on missing/ambiguous/unloadable designation, property-query
merge (enforcing and preferred), and a substitution regression that
plants a loadable same-named decoy on the default module search path and
asserts the configured-path activation fails closed rather than loading
it. Unit coverage also rejects identity alias collisions, while a CLI
recipe exercises the real library-init path and confirms a missing
designation aborts initialization rather than failing open. Ambiguous
dotted property-query mode entries are rejected so include order cannot
silently choose between enforcing and preferred policy.

Provider-agnostic: SymCrypt (or any provider) can be designated via the
same mechanism by naming it in [provider_sect] and [azl_fips_provider].

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
